<!--T:1-->
{{Macro
|Name=Cam Cycloid
|Description=This macro creates a new part that has a sketch of the profile of the mechanical cam. The input is the radius, the starting-angle and the end-angle of each dwell interval of the cam. The transition intervals of the cam are calculated using the cycloidal function, and approximated by B splines.
|Author=Garlic
|Download=[https://www.freecad.org/wiki/images/2/2c/Text-x-python.png Text-x-python.png]
|Links=[https://git.disroot.org/CrowdDiscussesAlternatives/ScriptsForFreeCAD/src/branch/master/CamCycloid/CamCycloid_documentation.pdf Cam Cycloid Documentation]
|Date=2026-06-01
|Version=0.0.1
|FCVersion= All
}}

==Installation== <!--T:2-->

<!--T:3-->
Please note that you need to do the following steps only once:
# From the menu of FreeCAD, go to {{MenuCommand|Macro → Macros}}
# Find which is the path written in the field '''User Macros Location'''
# Open this folder with the file explorer (or any other file management application).
# Download the file '''CamCycloid.py''' from https://git.disroot.org/CrowdDiscussesAlternatives/ScriptsForFreeCAD/src/branch/master/CamCycloid and paste it in this folder.
# {{Button|Close}} the [[Std_DlgMacroExecute|Execute Macro dialog box]].

<!--T:4-->
[[How_to_install_macros|Here]], you can find more info on how to manually install macros.

==Usage== <!--T:5-->

<!--T:6-->
The following examples demonstrate how to use this script.

===Example 1=== <!--T:7-->

====Executing the script==== <!--T:8-->

<!--T:9-->
In order for the algorithm to run, you need to open the [[Python_Console|Python Console]] on FreeCAD, and execute the code below (i.e. copy-paste each line of the code below separately and each time press {{KEY|Enter}}).

</translate>
{{Code|code=
import CamCycloid
camCycloidObj = CamCycloid.CamCycloid()
camCycloidObj.createCamProfile()
}}
<translate>

<!--T:10-->
Please note that once you open FreeCAD, you need to copy-paste the first two lines of this code only once (the first line imports the script and the second line creates an object). Then, every time you want to execute the algorithm of the script (in order to create a new part with the sketch of the profile of the cam), just copy-paste the third line of the code above and then press {{KEY|Enter}}.

<!--T:11-->
The following message will appear in the [[Python_Console|Python Console]]:

</translate>
<pre>
Please enter the number of knots (points) that the B-spline (which will approximate the cycloid curve) will have (i.e. number of steps in discretization). Number must be more than 2 (20 is a good choice):
</pre>
<translate>

<!--T:12-->
You can write {{Value|20}} and press {{KEY|Enter}}.

<!--T:13-->
Then, the following message appears in the python console:

</translate>
<pre>
Please enter the data of the cam in the format:
Radius_Of_1st_Dwell_Interval,Start_Angle_Of_1st_Dwell_Interval,End_Angle_Of_1st_Dwell_Interval,Radius_Of_2nd_Dwell_Interval,Start_Angle_Of_2nd_Dwell_Interval,End_Angle_Of_2nd_Dwell_Interval and so on, until you write as many dwell intervals the cam will have. Angles must be in degrees and radius in mm. Use dot for decimal separator. Positive angles are from X axis (x > 0) turning counterclockwise:
</pre>
<translate>

<!--T:14-->
In this example we will write the following input data:

<!--T:15-->
{{Value|60,20,70,80,200,250}}

<!--T:16-->
and then, when pressing {{KEY|Enter}}, a new part that has a body with a sketch is being created.

<!--T:17-->
When you open the sketch, the profile of the cam appears.

</translate>
[[File:CamCycloid_Macro_Example1_SketchOfMechCamProfile.png]]
<translate>

<!--T:18-->
The first dwell interval of the profile of the cam has a radius of 60 mm. It starts at an angle of 20° and it ends at an angle of 70° (with respect to x axis). During the rotation of the mechanical cam, when the cam-follower is in contact with the dwell interval of the cam, the stroke of the mechanism does not change (i.e. there is no motion when this part of the profile of the cam is in contact with the cam-follower).

</translate>
[[File:CamCycloid_Macro_Example1_1stDwellIntervalOfProfile.png]]
<translate>

<!--T:19-->
The second dwell interval of the profile of the cam has a radius of 80 mm. It starts at an angle of 200° and it ends at an angle of 250° (with respect to the positive direction of x axis, i.e. y = 0 and x > 0).

<!--T:20-->
Please note that when two dwell intervals are overlapping, then an error message appears in the [[Python_Console|Python Console]]:

</translate>
<pre>
Error: Dwell intervals are overlapping! Please check the angles of the dwell intervals.
</pre>
<translate>

<!--T:21-->
Similarly, the radius of a dwell interval must be always positive, and its end-angle always greater than its starting-angle.

====Cycloidal function for calculating the transition intervals==== <!--T:22-->

<!--T:23-->
The transition from the first dwell interval to the second dwell interval and from the second dwell interval to the first dwell interval is calculated using the cycloidal function:
</translate>
<!--
<math>yOfCycloid = \frac {stroke}{\pi} \left ( \pi \frac {thetaAngleInRad}{angleRangeInRad} – 0.5 \sin \left ( 2\pi \frac {thetaAngleInRad}{angleRangeInRad} \right ) \right )</math>
<math>\Leftrightarrow</math>
<math>yOfCycloid = stroke \frac {thetaAngleInRad}{angleRangeInRad} – \frac {stroke}{2\pi} \sin \left ( 2\pi \frac {thetaAngleInRad}{angleRangeInRad} \right )</math>
-->
<translate>

</translate>
{{Code|code=
yOfCycloid = stroke/math.pi * (math.pi*thetaAngleInRad/angleRangeInRad – 0.5*math.sin(2*math.pi*thetaAngleInRad/angleRangeInRad))
}}
<translate>

<!--T:24-->
<math>\Leftrightarrow</math>

</translate>
{{Code|code=
yOfCycloid = stroke*thetaAngleInRad/angleRangeInRad – stroke/(2*math.pi)*math.sin(2*math.pi*thetaAngleInRad/angleRangeInRad)
}}
<translate>

<!--T:25-->
where:
* {{incode|yOfCycloid}} is the displacement of the cam-follower (in mm) when the cam has rotated {{incode|thetaAngleInRad}} (which are shown in the picture below).
* The {{incode|stroke}} of the 1<sup>st</sup> transition interval is the difference between the radius of the second dwell interval and the radius of the first dwell interval.
* {{incode|AngleRangeInRad}} of the 1<sup>st</sup> transition interval is the difference between the starting-angle of the second dwell interval and the end-angle of the first dwell interval.

</translate>
[[File:CamCycloid_Macro_Example1_ThetaAngleOfTransitionInterval.png]]

[[File:CamCycloid_Macro_Example1_AngleRangeOf1stTransitionInterval.png]]
<translate>

<!--T:26-->
In this example, the stroke of the 1<sup>st</sup> transition interval is 20mm (i.e. 80mm – 60mm), and the angle range is 130° (i.e. 200° – 70°), which is equal to 2.268928 rad.

<!--T:27-->
For more information about the cycloidal function for a mechanical cam, you can visit the following link:

<!--T:28-->
https://mech.poriyaan.in/topic/cycloidal-follower-motion-31880

<!--T:29-->
The curve of the cycloidal function is approximated by a B-spline. The more knots you choose that the B-spline will have, the more accurate the approximation will be.

===Example 2=== <!--T:30-->

<!--T:31-->
In the [[Python_Console|Python Console]] write:

</translate>
{{Code|code=
camCycloidObj.createCamProfile()
}}
<translate>

<!--T:32-->
and press {{KEY|Enter}}.

<!--T:33-->
In the first prompt message write {{Value|20}} and press {{KEY|Enter}}.

<!--T:34-->
In the second prompt message write:

<!--T:35-->
{{Value|60,-30,20,80,150,200}}

<!--T:36-->
and then press {{KEY|Enter}}.

<!--T:37-->
You can see that in this case the x axis passes through the curve of the first dwell interval (since the starting angle is -30° with respect to the x axis). Keep in mind that the algorithm converts all the angles to positive angles and then reorders the dwell intervals (in ascending order), but this is not important unless you want to alter/improve the algorithm.

</translate>
[[File:CamCycloid_Macro_Example2_SketchOfMechCamProfile.png]]
<translate>

